WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.620
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
So the challenges

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.100
Wouter's team faces are not
exclusive to one exclusive.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.200
Capturing data from
different sources,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.880
performing various kinds of
analysis for different clients,

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.830
ensuring that the data
analysis is accurate, timely,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.140
and useful, this is common
challenge across the companies.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.330
GCP offers many solutions that
can help you tame this problem

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.520
and get the right set of
insights for your business.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.860
So let's dive in.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.730
When people say Google, first
instinct is to think of Search.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.240
And with our original mission
to organize the world's

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.910
information and make it
universally accessible,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.850
Google had to solve
many technical problems

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.320
to be able to reach--

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.110
to build a service that can
serve billions of people.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.090
Many of these
technical challenges

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.250
were necessary for search,
but they're actually

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.930
general purpose big
data challenges.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.290
So we had to solve
storage problem.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.820
We had the different
types of data,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.986
and we needed different
storage solutions for them--

00:01:03.986 --> 00:01:06.602
file systems, databases,
key value stores, et cetera.

00:01:06.602 --> 00:01:08.810
We needed to process this
data, and some of this data

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.320
needed to be processed as a
batch or as a streaming, right?

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.300
Like they have
different tradeoffs.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.600
And finally, we needed to figure
out how to serve our service.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.180
How can we deploy an
update running service

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.030
with no downtime?

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:26.420
So over the time, our
infrastructure evolved

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.500
and our business evolved.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.390
So now we have, I
think, eight services

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:32.400
that have a daily reach
more than a billion users.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.760
And with this growth, our
technical infrastructure

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.090
evolved as well.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.460
And we are now able to support
very different use cases.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.790
Yet all of these use cases still
share the same infrastructure,

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:48.230
hardware, networking,
and software stack.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.330
In the last 15 years,
we've published

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.640
about our infrastructure
and the tools

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.606
we've built, starting with GFS
and MapReduce papers, Bigtable

00:01:57.606 --> 00:01:58.980
Dremel, all the
way to DataPoint,

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.890
TensorFlow in the
last few years.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.030
We've actually changed
our Google infrastructure,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.560
but also influenced
the industry.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.039
Now, many of these products have
evolved into cloud offerings

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:11.810
and unlocking this
infrastructure

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.880
to our external customers.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.910
We have a variety of products in
compute, storage and databases

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.040
space, big data, and
machine learning.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.220
And in the next few slides,
I'll highlight the few that

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.700
are relevant to Wouter's case.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.780
So microservices, as you
heard, give you flexibility

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.730
to solve one problem at a time.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.550
They're easier to
develop, test, and deploy.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.620
That makes them popular choice.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.570
However, maintaining
microservices

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.750
is not a trivial job.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.100
You need to be able to
scale them, upgrade them

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.130
without downtime, and figure
out when things go wrong.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.020
So this is where
Google Container Engine

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.160
comes to the plate.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.250
Container Engine provides
a fully managed container

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.490
solution and allows
you to deploy

00:02:55.490 --> 00:03:01.140
your containerized workflows.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.160
So we get a powerful Cluster
Manager and orchestration

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.810
system that is built on top
of open source Kubernetes.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.980
It has a private
Container Registry,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.130
so you have your
own microservices

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.750
well-defined and hidden.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.280
Convenient and
powerful declarative

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.460
API for resource specification.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.010
And because it's built
on Kubernetes, which

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.860
is open source, and there
are many other partners

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.840
and companies out there
who are using this,

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.340
it actually allows you to
combine both public cloud

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.930
and on-premise solutions.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.230
So using something
like Container Engine

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.600
and Kubernetes, you can
have a hybrid cloud.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.550
The data warehousing is a
huge part of every business.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:51.620
And SQL is the interface
of choice to this dataset.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.970
In Google Cloud Platform,
if you have datasets up

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:59.800
to 10 terabytes, you can
use Google Cloud SQL.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.370
With it, you get a fully
managed and reliable SQL service

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.750
built on Postgres or MySQL that
comes with automatic failover,

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:10.880
and optionally, high
availability mode.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.310
You do not have to
upgrade your database.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.640
You do not have to
monitor it all the time.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.279
We ensure that nothing
happens to your machine,

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:20.860
and the things just run.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.340
It's a performance
and scalable up

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.750
to 10 terabytes, 25,000
IOPS, and it comes

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.370
with automatic encryption.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.920
And you have full control
over firewall settings

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.520
and you can protect your
access to the database.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.730
Being MySQL and Postgres, it
has a strong partner ecosystem.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:43.400
If your dataset grows to
more than 10 terabytes

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.490
and you still want to use SQL,
we do have solution for that.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.680
And it's Google Cloud BigQuery.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.750
BigQuery is the fully
managed, low cost

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.400
serverless data warehouse.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.350
You never see the machines.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.580
You just go to the website
and enter your queries.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.040
It exposes SQL interface.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.980
It has a seamless scalability
from gigabytes to petabytes.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.310
And it's secure and reliable.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.390
Again, automatic encryption,
automatic replication.

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:11.000
All of this happens
behind the curtains.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.140
You don't see it.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.190
You just use the service.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.460
However, even with BigQuery
scale and performance,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.380
there are problems
that require sort

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.100
of guaranteeing low latency and
consistent throughput, which

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.760
is hard to guarantee
with a SQL service.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.420
So for these cases, we
have Google Cloud Bigtable.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.000
This is a NoSQL
big data database

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.940
that powers many of the
products you probably use--

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.800
Search, Analytics, Maps, Gmail.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.150
It's behind all of that.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:45.750
It's, again, fully
managed NoSQL database.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.150
You don't get to see
the machines, again,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.860
for all practical purposes.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:55.500
It has petascale scalability
with very low latency.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.320
And it can grow the
throughput as needed.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:00.840
The interesting
thing with Bigtable

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.520
is, depending on
your access patterns,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.990
it learns and adjusts to them.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:09.610
So over the time, your serving
becomes more efficient.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:11.802
So we looked at some of
the storage solutions.

00:06:11.802 --> 00:06:12.760
Now you have this data.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:13.960
You want to process it.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:18.372
Many of you are coming from
existing frameworks that

00:06:18.372 --> 00:06:20.080
may have been built
on open source tools,

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.660
like Apache Hadoop or Spark.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.450
We want you to be able
to reuse these tools

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:28.480
in your applications, so we
have a Google Cloud Dataproc

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.830
offering.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:31.950
And this is stateless--

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.670
Dataproc gives you
stateless clusters

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.310
that can spin up in
seconds, up to a minute.

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:39.190
And can read and write the
data to various sources,

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.910
like GCS, Bigtable,
BigQuery, with HDFS and HBASE

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.890
compatible connectors.

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.730
It also provides convenient
API for management

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.070
of both clusters and jobs.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.320
So with Dataproc, you
can choose whether you

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.090
want to run your
Dataproc cluster

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:58.510
as a long-living cluster
with HDFS and everything

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.210
in it on it, like
you did on-premise

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:02.740
or on other cloud provider.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.190
Or you can take a
benefit of a low latency

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:08.050
that we have for
starting clusters

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.150
and run a job per
cluster, and just

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.700
bring down the cluster
when your job is done.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.650
So Dataproc is great, but
we learned a few things

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.300
in data processing in Google.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.644
So petabyte-scale
processing engine Google way

00:07:22.644 --> 00:07:23.560
is our Cloud Dataflow.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:29.300
The Dataflow took many
of the internal learnings

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.190
and turned them into
a data processing

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:35.300
solution that scales both for
batch and streaming workloads.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.530
It is fully managed and
[INAUDIBLE] configured.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.940
You can start Dataflow jobs
with not specifying anything

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.430
much than code.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:48.530
And it provides unified batch
and stream processing model.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.010
So you write your data
transformations once,

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.260
and depending on input source
and output source that you use,

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.220
we have a streaming pipeline
or we have a batch pipeline.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:08:00.080
So if you're consuming
Pub/Sub, voila,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.250
you are having streaming.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:05.220
If you're consuming GCS,
you are reading the batch.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.880
And we open sourced
the Dataflow SDK

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.880
and turned it into Apache
Beam project in the last year.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.950
So we scale to petabytes
of data, millions of QPS

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:16.700
automatically.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.640
And we invested a lot of time
in high quality integrations

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.070
with GCP.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.750
Oops.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.930
Google Cloud Platform
ensures seamless integration

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:26.930
of these powerful tools.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:29.600
You can use any one of them
and be perfectly happy.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.280
But the real power
of the platform

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.340
comes from using them
together and providing

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.950
comprehensive
solution that's well

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.870
integrated with monitoring,
debugging, alerting,

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.330
and all these other things.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:44.080
And this is where GCP excels.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.590
So with this, let's go back
to Business Intelligence case.

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:50.120
WOUTER CUYPERS: Thank you,
Jelena, for the great overview.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.460
So we ended with this slide.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.190
So it's a quick recap
of where we want to go.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.940
I'm going to start with
our original architecture,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.130
like I encountered in
2013 when I started,

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:02.390
and how it still lives.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:06.140
So we have a monolithic .Net
application on top over SQL

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.230
cluster.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:11.210
And for reporting purposes,
we copy the data every night

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.210
to a separate reporting
database so that the analysts

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.440
don't bring down production.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:16.730
That's pretty obvious.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:20.990
Most of the analysis happens in
Excel using queries that they

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:24.580
write directly on the database.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.980
And then me and Alex,
my tech lead, came in,

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:32.210
and we started to push most
of our really large datasets

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:34.160
into the cloud, into BigQuery.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.480
Mostly this is email.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.160
We send about 2
million emails per day

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.290
and we have about five rows of
data, potentially, per email,

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.630
so that's quite a lot of rows.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:46.040
Click tracking also up to 20
million rows on a single day.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:50.100
SQL couldn't manage that,
but BigQuery does it fine.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.000
We also copy production
data through Cloud Storage

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.080
into BigQuery so that our
analysts can enrich the data

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:00.530
that they get from these streams
with the data from production.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:04.550
Of course, with
one day of delay.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.220
We have adopted
Tableau as the new tool

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.370
to analyze data as a
replacement to Excel.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:16.200
And it's bringing the data that
we have in SQL and in BigQuery

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:19.010
to pretty much everyone in
the company through very nice

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:19.890
looking dashboards.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:24.790
So we're already
quite data driven.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:29.160
Lastly, personalization
is also a part of my team.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.640
It's what our data
scientists do.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.380
And they run it in PySpark
on top of Cloud Dataproc.

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:35.230
So we are using the
stateless version

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:36.880
of what Jelena explained.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:37.580
We provision.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:40.180
We spin up a cluster.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.860
At night, we do
our calculations,

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:46.430
which import data from
Cloud Storage and BigQuery.

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:49.550
And the results go back
into Cloud Storage.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:52.170
And then we copy it
back over to production.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:55.130
This is a big pain point,
because we generate

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:58.010
a lot of data out of Spark.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.650
This can be, really,
millions of rows.

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:02.610
And the copying takes
quite a long time.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.965
So this is a really
big pain point.

00:11:05.965 --> 00:11:07.840
To the new architecture,
then, because that's

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:09.860
why you're here for.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:13.340
The new architecture will
be angular front ends on top

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:18.410
of .Net core microservices
running in containerized Linux

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.110
environment in Kubernetes.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:23.630
All of them have their
own MongoDB database.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:27.200
So NoSQL, or in some
cases, Cloud SQL.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:28.880
I'm not going to
go into much detail

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:32.192
about Kubernetes and containers,
because I'm like the BI guy.

00:11:32.192 --> 00:11:33.650
But if you have
any questions, feel

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:37.430
free to come up after the show.

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:40.299
This is a very basic back office
screen like we have it today.

00:11:40.299 --> 00:11:41.840
I hope you can read
it, I'm not sure.

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:44.270
But what this shows
is for employees

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:46.400
inside one of those
deals that we have,

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.760
all of the products, their
name, price, stock information,

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.767
how many orders we've had
so far, how many views

00:11:52.767 --> 00:11:54.350
we had so that they
can have some kind

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.790
of an indication of conversion.

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:58.190
And they use this to
intervene in live sales.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:01.190
Now, to make this screen
in our new environment,

00:12:01.190 --> 00:12:04.210
the UI will have to contact
four different microservices

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:05.120
and BigQuery.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:06.822
It's going to be
horribly complicated.

00:12:06.822 --> 00:12:08.780
So my team said, look,
we will just provide you

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.850
with a microservice
which gives all of that

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.370
data as a single JSON
payload, and we'll

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:16.830
figure out where to get the
data and how to merge it.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.220
So how do we get the data?

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:21.080
Microservices, obviously,
they talk to each other.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.226
They use direct endpoint calls,
but also asynchronous events.

00:12:25.226 --> 00:12:26.850
So let's say you have
a member service.

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:27.970
A new member registers.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:30.800
It's sent an event and some
kind of other service which

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.870
is responsible for sending
out our welcome mail

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:36.320
says, hey, new member,
I'm going to send a mail

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.730
without needing a direct call.

00:12:38.730 --> 00:12:40.970
Now, we require all
of our microservices

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.980
to send out events
for the entities

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:46.620
that they manage internally,
being it a product, an article,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.120
a member, an order.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:50.340
And each time these things
get created or modified,

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:53.090
we send events
over Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.730
And all of those events
are read by Dataflow,

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.920
and stored in the Cloud
Bigtable so that my team's BI

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.040
environment has a
perfect copy of what is

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.620
happening in the microservices.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.690
How do we do that in practice?

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:07.640
We use Google protocol
buffers, which

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.400
is a very nice technology for
making these kinds of events.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:12.330
So you define them as a schema.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.350
And from that schema, you
generate the serialization code

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:18.200
in most common
programming languages.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.470
And they end up being
serialized in very small,

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:25.570
very efficient binary files,
which go over to Pub/Sub bus,

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.330
are read by our
streaming Dataflow,

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.930
and we just store them
in Bigtable as binary.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:31.910
So no deserialization
happens in this flow.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:34.910
It's actually a
very simple flow.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:37.650
Bigtable is really nice,
because as Jelena said,

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:39.500
you can reach very high
throughput, very low

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:41.840
latency, which is what we need.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.120
Because we are going to
be firing a lot of events.

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:46.580
And a nice feature
as well is that when

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:50.390
you write a cell into
BigQuery, it's timestamped.

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.570
And every older version
of that cell is kept.

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:57.220
So we not only have a copy of
our microservice environment,

00:13:57.220 --> 00:13:59.730
but we also know the entire
history of everything

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:01.610
that ever happens.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.570
So all of the email address
changes that users did,

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:06.752
changes to orders,
modifications to addresses,

00:14:06.752 --> 00:14:07.960
all of those things are kept.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:12.641
We can roll things back and we
can replay history if we want.

00:14:12.641 --> 00:14:13.890
How do we bring the data back?

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:15.370
Bigtable is not for reporting.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:16.670
It's a key value store.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:21.530
So we use our own microservices
written in Python or Google Go

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:23.430
to read the data out
of Bigtable and serve

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:28.730
it to back office screens, other
microservices where needed.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:31.490
The really large data
streams, as before,

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:33.020
go straight into BigQuery.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:37.680
It doesn't make sense to
fire those into Bigtable.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.680
So we've got our data
in MongoDB, Cloud SQL.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.060
We've got our external
data in BigQuery.

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:47.170
What we want in the end is
analyst data and production

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:47.670
data.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:50.320
So the production data is to
do things like that back office

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:51.820
screen that I showed you.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:55.970
And analyst data is the raw
entity data-- all of our users,

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.641
all of our orders,
all of our returns.

00:14:57.641 --> 00:15:00.140
But also, that preprocessed
data that we were talking about.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.900
So the turnover we did
on a sale last year

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:04.555
and unique visitors we had.

00:15:04.555 --> 00:15:05.430
So how do we do that?

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.430
How do we use the
intermediate technologies,

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:10.740
like Pub/Sub, Dataflow,
and Bigtable to get there?

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.310
Well, it depends a
bit on the scenario,

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:17.660
but we see like four
patterns or strategies

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:20.480
that we use in
combination to get there.

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.575
So one of them, I have two real
time strategies and two batch

00:15:23.575 --> 00:15:25.566
mode strategies.

00:15:25.566 --> 00:15:28.190
The first real time strategy is
to read from different Pub/Subs

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:31.130
at the same time, combining
the data in Dataflow,

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.375
and bring that to Bigtable.

00:15:33.375 --> 00:15:36.424
An example, we get
new users in everyday,

00:15:36.424 --> 00:15:38.090
but our analysts don't
just want to know

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:39.230
how many new users we have.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:41.229
They also want to know,
where did they come from

00:15:41.229 --> 00:15:42.840
and what kind of
device did they use?

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:45.500
The member service will send
us an event about the new user,

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.250
but that doesn't contain
that information.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:48.750
That they need from
the clickstream,

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.100
which is another Pub/Sub stream.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:51.426
So we read both.

00:15:51.426 --> 00:15:52.800
We combine the
data, and we store

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.354
that final output in Bigtable.

00:15:54.354 --> 00:15:56.270
We need to do it like
that, because when we're

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.890
reading the
clickstream, we can't

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:02.390
assume that the member entity
will already be in Bigtable.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.850
Another strategy is to
read data from Pub/Sub,

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.810
process it in Cloud Dataflow,
and load site information

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.040
from Bigtable.

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:12.500
For example, when you want to
calculate the unique visitors

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:15.170
per country, our
clickstream tells us

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.669
that who is clicking
where, but it

00:16:17.669 --> 00:16:19.710
doesn't tell us what the
country of that user is.

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:22.405
So we ask Bigtable to
tell us the country.

00:16:22.405 --> 00:16:25.860
We generate our KPI, and
we store it in Bigtable.

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.440
So that's the second
strategy that I

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:30.910
will demonstrate at the
end if I have enough time.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:32.300
That screen that
we talked about,

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:34.160
that back office screen,
will actually already

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:35.618
be a combination
of both strategies

00:16:35.618 --> 00:16:37.726
to make that possible.

00:16:37.726 --> 00:16:39.440
Then batch mode.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:42.770
Here we will read data,
mostly from BigQuery, also

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:43.880
from Bigtable.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.490
We combine it, calculate
it in Cloud Dataflow,

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.920
and the end result goes
back into Cloud Bigtable.

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:51.711
If we do a lot of processing
for various kinds of reports,

00:16:51.711 --> 00:16:53.960
things that people often
need, that the analysts needs

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.570
pre-calculated, that will
be mostly this example.

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:58.260
For example, an email
campaign report.

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.980
That's a lot of email click
data that comes from BigQuery.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:02.970
It's enriched because
this data is pretty bare,

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:04.166
the email tracking data.

00:17:04.166 --> 00:17:05.540
Enriched with
member information,

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:07.579
and the results
go into Bigtable.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:11.060
And that's before the
data science stuff goes

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:12.680
into stateless Cloud Dataproc.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.010
But instead of reading
from SQL, as we do today,

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:17.450
we read from Cloud Bigtable.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:20.530
And the results also go
back to Cloud Bigtable

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:23.720
so that we can very
fastly provision

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:26.960
this data into production
without needing to copy it over

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:31.240
again to an SQL server
as we did before.

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.840
Where the data ends up in the
end depends on who needs it.

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:37.144
If we need it in the back
office or in production,

00:17:37.144 --> 00:17:38.560
we serve it through
a microservice

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.560
and the data
resides in Bigtable.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:45.190
If our analysts need it, we
create a dataset in Bigtable,

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:49.040
and depending on the frequency
required-- hourly, daily,

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:49.720
weekly--

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.920
we use a Dataflow to stream
this data into BigQuery or Cloud

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:53.830
SQL.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.570
Well, both, actually,
so that people

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.744
can join the stuff in BigQuery
with these huge datasets.

00:17:58.744 --> 00:18:00.160
Because the really
large datasets,

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.140
like our clickstream
and email stream,

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:04.450
we will just enrich
them with Bigtable data

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:08.420
and store the result in BigQuery
as really large, denormalized

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.970
datasets.

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.040
What if things go wrong?

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:14.850
And they do, and they will.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.060
We back up all of our events.

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:19.990
So all of the events are
also read from Pub/Sub

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:22.540
by a dedicated backup
streaming dataflow, which

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.870
stores all of them, again,
as binary into BigQuery.

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:27.800
There's an additional
batch job which copies

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:29.867
it to cold storage
just in case somebody

00:18:29.867 --> 00:18:31.450
wants to delete the
table in BigQuery.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:37.810
Restoring happens by using
the same dataflows, streaming

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:39.610
or batch, doesn't
matter, a streaming

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:40.990
can be run as batch too.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:45.340
And we change the input from
originally Pub/Sub to BigQuery,

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:49.110
which contains exactly the same
binary protocol buffer files.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:50.890
And we store the
corrected information

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:54.280
again in Cloud Bigtable.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:55.640
So now for the demo.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:56.850
Do we have time?

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:58.340
Yes, we have time.

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:02.200
So this is a very
simple dataflow

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:04.729
that I've built specifically
for showing it here

00:19:04.729 --> 00:19:06.770
to demonstrate a little
bit of what we are doing.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:10.240
So it's on our actual production
data today, because all of what

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.860
I've been explaining to
you, it's not done yet.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:14.097
We're building it.

00:19:14.097 --> 00:19:16.180
But we've, of course,
tested a lot of these things

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:17.470
conceptually.

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:19.510
So I'm going to write
our current production

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:23.620
data as event sources
over Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:25.847
I'm going to count them,
but from the clickstream,

00:19:25.847 --> 00:19:27.430
I don't know the
country, so I'm going

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:30.190
to get the country
from Bigtable.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:32.500
And I'm going to store the
result as a time series,

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:34.000
again, in Cloud Bigtable.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:37.750
And I will use a simple
Node.js bare bones web service

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:41.810
to show that on
the browser screen.

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:43.950
Let's have a look.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:47.692
Can I switch to the computer?

00:19:47.692 --> 00:19:48.540
Oh, yeah.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:53.370
So when you load your
dataflow in Dataflow,

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.330
in the interface and
the [INAUDIBLE] online,

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:58.630
you get a very nice overview in
blocks of what your pipeline is

00:19:58.630 --> 00:19:59.530
actually doing.

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:01.540
So it starts with
reading from Pub/Sub.

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:06.280
It's windowing it, in this
case, in one minute windows.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.240
We do some internal
conversion to a clause

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.520
that we can use in Java.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:10.480
We filter it.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.790
And then we count the views.

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:15.520
And within that, we first get
that country from Bigtable.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:18.700
And now we count
the actual views.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:20.830
That gets converted into
a mutation, which is then

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.547
finally committed to Bigtable.

00:20:22.547 --> 00:20:23.880
An the interface is really nice.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:28.010
You can see how many
workers are doing the job.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.436
Can you actually read that?

00:20:29.436 --> 00:20:31.510
Yes, [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:33.910
You can shake logs,
look for exceptions

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:35.410
if anything is going wrong.

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:37.020
It's a really nice interface.

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:40.640
Now, Pub/Sub, I can't
show you much about that.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:42.800
But what I can show
you is Stackdriver,

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:45.880
which shows us how many
events are coming in.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:49.030
So currently, we have
a good 100 events

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:51.160
per second going through--

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:53.969
well, going through
the Pub/Sub channel.

00:20:53.969 --> 00:20:56.260
If I show you that per day,
you see the typical morning

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.690
and evening peak
that our website has.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:00.160
So we're seeing events.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.526
Now, what kind of
events are those?

00:21:02.526 --> 00:21:04.870
This is our website.

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:09.525
Let's say I'm
interested in shoes.

00:21:09.525 --> 00:21:11.140
I'm actually doing
this over a phone,

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:14.902
because the wi-fi wouldn't
cooperate, so bear with me.

00:21:14.902 --> 00:21:18.330
So I'm going to
show a few products.

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:19.886
Put one in my cart.

00:21:19.886 --> 00:21:22.750
Now, with BigQuery,
they look like this.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:25.256
I'm going to refresh this.

00:21:25.256 --> 00:21:27.680
BigQuery is really nice
for these furry details.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:32.154
So this is a table of probably
a good 10 million rows now.

00:21:32.154 --> 00:21:33.820
It has some setup
time in the beginning,

00:21:33.820 --> 00:21:35.236
because your query
gets dispatched

00:21:35.236 --> 00:21:38.767
over x amount of machines, and
then the results come back.

00:21:38.767 --> 00:21:39.850
So it takes a few seconds.

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:42.310
But then it goes very quickly.

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:46.360
So you see here I have a product
fuse I would like updated.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:48.980
I've devised identifiers,
session identifiers.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.310
This is that user identifier
that I will be using.

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:54.010
And we also stored some
additional unstructured data

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:56.650
in JSON, mostly
to optimize things

00:21:56.650 --> 00:22:00.340
like recommender
engines and such things.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:04.420
So finally, what is
this going to look like?

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:06.010
I can't show you
much of Bigtable,

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:09.670
but this is how it looks if you
talk to it using your command

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:10.545
line interface.

00:22:10.545 --> 00:22:12.880
So I'm going to get
the last few events.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:15.148
And here you see my time series.

00:22:15.148 --> 00:22:16.064
Oh, can you read that?

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:20.120
Here you see my time series.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.950
So you see events, the page
views for Belgium, Luxembourg,

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:27.100
and Netherlands being
written per minute.

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:28.090
And with a timestamp.

00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:31.150
So you see when the data was
being written into Bigtable.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:36.520
So this is how it looks like.

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.120
So you see my one
minute time series

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.520
that was generated by Dataflow.

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:44.340
And to show you the real
real-timeness of the thing,

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.120
I've instructed Dataflow
to update my latest

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:48.630
window every few seconds.

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:51.240
So these are the actual events
as coming on our website

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:52.096
right now.

00:22:52.096 --> 00:22:53.840
It's not fake.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:54.570
And voila!

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.100
This gives you an
idea of how you

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.950
can use Cloud Dataflow to
use these kinds of things.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:22:59.700
So back to Jelena.

00:23:03.604 --> 00:23:06.044
OK, can I go back to the slides?

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:15.700
OK, thank you, Wouter.

