The question was, do you have,
on top of the technical stack

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:34.865
itself, big [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:34.865 --> 00:24:39.140
Do you have these
business scenarios or use

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:43.010
cases somehow captured into,
say, templatized formats,

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:46.960
like certain business cases or
specific types of use cases?

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.470
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
We do have

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:50.230
a couple of things for this.

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:56.900
On cloud.google.com/bigdata,
there are templated examples

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.520
of different business use cases.

00:24:58.520 --> 00:24:59.767
I think we have six or nine.

00:24:59.767 --> 00:25:01.600
I know there are three
a column, but I don't

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:03.900
know how many rows we have.

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:06.770
All of them have
a code in GitHub,

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:09.890
so you can actually see
how the code looks like.

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:13.460
And many of them are integration
between the different products,

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:16.640
because it's like
in a big data space.

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:19.460
We also have a ton
of datasets that

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:22.020
are available through
BigQuery or on GCS

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:24.020
that you can use
to sort of explore

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:26.600
the ideas that you have.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.820
So yeah, so there is some
documentation and some

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:30.830
of the examples there.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:34.640
We also offer some
trainings for these things.

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:37.490
And I believe if you
are interested in that,

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:38.870
I can find more information.

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:41.210
I don't know it
on top of my head.

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:44.090
But we definitely
continually build

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:49.250
this database of the
white papers and examples.

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:52.370
And I think very recently we
had a-- like I think this week,

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:55.400
or Friday last week, we
had a publication by one

00:25:55.400 --> 00:26:00.200
of our SMEs, Subject
Matter Experts, [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:03.490
on the best practices when
using the big data tools.

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:05.100
And that will be ongoing series.

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:07.200
So he published the
first part last week.

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:12.870
AUDIENCE: So thanks
for your presentation.

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:16.490
It may be a question for Wouter.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:21.710
How do you deal with personally
identifiable information that

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.920
comes from your
website in regards

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:29.420
to the nature of Bigtable, which
stores every record and also

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:32.780
every mutation of every
record, especially now

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:37.070
with GDPR coming up?

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:38.990
So my question is very general.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.610
How do you deal with that?

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:42.510
WOUTER CUYPERS: It's
a very good question.

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:44.630
And we've thought
about it a lot.

00:26:44.630 --> 00:26:47.750
What Cloud Platform really
allows you to do really well

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:52.290
is set permissions on
a technology level.

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.680
So each service, you can
keep it away from people.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:56.300
You can use specific
service accounts.

00:26:56.300 --> 00:27:00.620
So we don't want any analysts
talking to our Bigtable.

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:04.800
We also don't want
any other developers

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.100
talking to our Bigtable,
or other microservices.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:10.340
We keep that inside
our own team.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:16.110
And we get to say what we export
to the databases that allow

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:17.797
them to generate reporting.

00:27:17.797 --> 00:27:19.130
Because they can't use Bigtable.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:22.400
They can only use
Cloud SQL, or BigQuery.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:26.000
So there we can use
separate tables.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.400
So that's a bit the idea
that we're going to use.

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:31.790
We're going to put all of
the information that's really

00:27:31.790 --> 00:27:34.610
personally identifiable
in different tables,

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.470
and they would need
to join it together

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:37.880
to know who it really is.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:42.550
And only some people, like in
customer care, can do that.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:44.210
It will be done in
their back office.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:45.410
They will get the
rights to do that,

00:27:45.410 --> 00:27:46.962
but most people
will not be able to.

00:27:46.962 --> 00:27:48.920
Because you don't need
to know that information

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:50.692
to do most of your work anyway.

00:27:50.692 --> 00:27:52.483
Is that kind of an
answer to your question?

00:27:52.483 --> 00:27:56.047
AUDIENCE: It is, but then I
have a question for Jelena.

00:27:56.047 --> 00:27:57.213
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC: OK.

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:02.240
AUDIENCE: Thanks again.

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:06.140
So is there-- can you
specify in Bigtable

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:08.367
the type of retention period?

00:28:08.367 --> 00:28:11.730
So the GDPR specifies
that some type of data you

00:28:11.730 --> 00:28:16.950
will have to delete in maybe
two years or five years,

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:19.700
depending on the type of data.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.400
Can you set a retention
period, or is that

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:27.290
a thing you have to add a
little process in your flow

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:28.165
to take care of that?

00:28:28.165 --> 00:28:30.539
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC: So
that's an excellent question.

00:28:30.539 --> 00:28:33.250
So do we have capabilities to
automatically remove the data

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:35.120
after certain period of time?

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:39.800
And I would have to look up what
at Bigtable exactly it sets.

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:42.860
I know we do have these supports
for these things, for example,

00:28:42.860 --> 00:28:43.980
in Dataflow.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:46.250
And we have services
for this in Google.

00:28:46.250 --> 00:28:49.280
So you register, and if
the information is deleted,

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.570
you have to yank it
out of your system.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:54.710
So specifically for
Bigtable, I don't know,

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:56.990
but I can look it up.

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:59.630
But we definitely
care about the data,

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:04.400
and will are very well
aware of the data policies.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:05.960
So it's something we care about.

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:07.730
WOUTER CUYPERS:
But on the GDPR, we

00:29:07.730 --> 00:29:10.510
are currently working
with consultants

00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:12.530
who are specialized
in this, and even they

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:14.480
don't know how it's going to be.

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:16.940
So it's going to be
with the EU cookie law.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.190
Some big players are going
to make the first moves,

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:22.110
and the rest will kind
of standardize on that.

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:23.911
And it's also not
that clear cut.

00:29:23.911 --> 00:29:25.910
You don't have to delete
the data, they tell us.

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:27.170
You have to anonymize it.

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:29.600
So you have to delete someone's
name and email address,

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:32.240
but you can still
keep views and orders.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:34.657
And some data you will even
need to keep, like an invoice.

00:29:34.657 --> 00:29:36.073
You will need to
keep that forever

00:29:36.073 --> 00:29:37.850
in your database, which
is also personally

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:39.290
identifiable information.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.910
So it's much more
complicated than that.

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:44.310
AUDIENCE: It's true, while
being in a big player,

00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:46.710
we tend to be the [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:51.930
WOUTER CUYPERS: For you.

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:57.785
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
Any other questions?

00:29:57.785 --> 00:29:59.100
AUDIENCE: Yeah, OK.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:01.710
So my question is for Wouter.

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:03.450
I was listening to
your presentation

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:07.710
and I think it's nice, but
what for me was missing

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:10.890
was that I want to believe
that this journey has not

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:12.470
been all roses.

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:15.450
And if there has
been any hurdles

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:17.340
that you've encountered
in this process

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:19.920
that you want to
share that would

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:24.080
be as an example for anybody who
wants to be on this migration

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:25.121
journey.

00:30:25.121 --> 00:30:27.120
WOUTER CUYPERS: I think
one of the main hurdles,

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:29.550
if you see what kind of
evolution we went through,

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:34.320
is getting to grips with the
different breeds of technology

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:36.030
that Google has.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:39.180
Bigtable is really a very
different kind of database

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:41.230
than an SQL database.

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:45.420
And if you look at it,
it looks as if you simply

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:46.649
can't do much with Bigtable.

00:30:46.649 --> 00:30:48.690
You read rows, you scan
rows, and you write them.

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:49.500
And that's it.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:52.860
But their schema design is
absolutely essential for what

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:53.940
you want to do.

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:57.450
So you need to spend more time
thinking about such things.

00:30:57.450 --> 00:30:59.610
And there's also
a lot of mistakes

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.290
that you make,
beginner mistakes.

00:31:02.290 --> 00:31:05.130
The first dataflows
that we wrote, yeah,

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:06.284
that didn't go over roses.

00:31:06.284 --> 00:31:08.200
We needed to get to grips
with the technology.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:11.100
So you need to invest a
lot of time in learning.

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:13.860
And I hope your company
allows you to do that,

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:18.075
because you will spend some
time trying things out.

00:31:18.075 --> 00:31:19.950
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
But presumably, it's

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:21.610
worth it, right?

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:23.230
WOUTER CUYPERS: Absolutely.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:25.636
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
OK, there is a question.

00:31:25.636 --> 00:31:27.070
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:32.810 --> 00:31:35.790
Wouter, thank you for
your presentation.

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:42.900
How big is your team which
thought this through and build

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:45.040
the implementations?

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:46.790
WOUTER CUYPERS: The
complete IT department

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.130
is between 40 and 50 people.

00:31:49.130 --> 00:31:50.060
I don't know exactly.

00:31:50.060 --> 00:31:53.510
And my department is
just seven people.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:55.400
But I think there
were some key people

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:59.810
in there, our architects, like
Alex, who's here's up front.

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:02.730
It's his brainchild that
I'm presenting here.

00:32:02.730 --> 00:32:03.840
So he did a marvelous job.

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:06.710
So if you want to congratulate
him or ask him more in-depth

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:10.550
questions, he's actually one
of the only Google development

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:11.690
experts in Belgium--

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:12.823
or the only one for Cloud.

00:32:12.823 --> 00:32:13.781
AUDIENCE: Not any more.

00:32:13.781 --> 00:32:15.060
WOUTER CUYPERS: Not any more?

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:15.893
And he works for us.

00:32:15.893 --> 00:32:18.610
We're very happy with that.

00:32:18.610 --> 00:32:20.101
AUDIENCE: That helps a lot.

00:32:20.101 --> 00:32:21.100
WOUTER CUYPERS: It does.

00:32:21.100 --> 00:32:21.975
AUDIENCE: OK, thanks.

00:32:33.260 --> 00:32:35.000
AUDIENCE: Question for Wouter.

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:38.250
Why and how did you
choose Google Cloud?

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:39.512
What was the decision tree?

00:32:39.512 --> 00:32:40.970
WOUTER CUYPERS:
Very good question.

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:43.502
Now, as you've seen,
we're not a big company.

00:32:43.502 --> 00:32:44.210
We're not a bank.

00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:47.180
We're not a big retailer.

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:49.160
And when I came
into the company,

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:50.510
we were only like 90 people.

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:51.920
We're three times that.

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:54.320
So it was a 20 people IT team.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:58.680
And yeah, two guys taking
care of a little bit of BI.

00:32:58.680 --> 00:33:01.610
So we had no time or
resources to compare

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:04.920
20 different providers,
technologies, and whatnot.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:08.910
So the decision was
based on a few things.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:11.780
One of them was we had a very
good relationship with Google

00:33:11.780 --> 00:33:14.992
because we were a
top AdWords client.

00:33:14.992 --> 00:33:16.700
We've got people with
a lot of experience

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.262
with Google
technology, like Alex.

00:33:19.262 --> 00:33:20.720
But I think one of
the main reasons

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:23.720
was that Google has these
technologies that Jelena

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:24.920
has been showing us.

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:25.820
Things like BigQuery.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:29.780
I mean, three years ago we
were baffled by the power

00:33:29.780 --> 00:33:30.796
that thing has.

00:33:30.796 --> 00:33:33.140
And this pushed
us over the edge.

00:33:33.140 --> 00:33:34.640
And also, the fact
that everything

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:36.800
can be done in a managed way.

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:40.610
I mean, you can run
Redshift, you can run Kafka.

00:33:40.610 --> 00:33:42.800
But you still have to
do a lot of management

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:46.100
of all of the servers that
run below these things.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.780
And we simply do not have
the resources to do that.

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:51.620
If we need to do that ourselves,
it will take us two years just

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:53.000
to build the infrastructure.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.610
So that was one of
the key reasons for us

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:58.077
to do it like this, because
with a very small team,

00:33:58.077 --> 00:33:59.410
we can do really powerful stuff.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:08.157
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
Any other questions?

00:34:08.157 --> 00:34:09.564
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:34:12.807 --> 00:34:13.890
WOUTER CUYPERS: I'm sorry.

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:14.949
AUDIENCE: Can you use--

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:16.480
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
Can you use the mic?

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:16.810
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:18.070
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
Thank you.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:20.570
AUDIENCE: Did you make use of
a Google [INAUDIBLE] or was it

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:22.778
completely your team that--

00:34:22.778 --> 00:34:25.190
WOUTER CUYPERS: For this, not.

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:27.150
Though for the
original architecture,

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:28.870
we did kind of--
well, the first steps

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:30.995
into BigQuery two years
ago, we talked a little bit

00:34:30.995 --> 00:34:33.430
to Datatonic, who's
here and has a booth.

00:34:33.430 --> 00:34:40.090
And I think went into Cloud
Data Platform because of us.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:42.280
So we had some chats
straight in the beginning

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.737
to confirm some of the big
bets that we were taking.

00:34:45.737 --> 00:34:46.570
But that's about it.

00:34:46.570 --> 00:34:49.000
The rest is made inhouse.

00:34:53.985 --> 00:34:55.360
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC:
OK, well

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:56.710
thank you everyone for coming.

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:58.510
And you can grab us after this.

